chapter Root
  chapter Philosophy
    text
      I believe programming should be more personal. Currently (2026), software
      tools are mostly developed by a few people in a central place and then
      used by many more people. However, you very rarely modify or customize
      your software tools. Even experienced programmers using open-source tools
      rarely look at the source code.
    text
      The current state is practical: It takes a lot of effort to build
      software, so naturally you need to bundle resources to create big
      projects. I like that this necessity to collaborate fosters social
      relationships and gets people to explore problem spaces together. However,
      there is also a place for more personal, customized, specialized, smaller
      software. This vision existed since the early days of computing: Alan Kay
      envisioned computers as "personal dynamic media", where every user feels
      comfortable to create, use, and customize tools. Orchard is my attempt at
      creating a personal computing medium.
  chapter Core
    chapter Nothing
      text
        Nothing is a type with only one instance. You might know it from other
        languages as unit or void. From an information theoretical perspective,
        being given an instance of a Nothing type gives you no information
        whatsoever.
        Nothing is used whenever we don't actually want to provide a useful
        value. Functions that only have side-effects (aka they may crash or hang
        but that's all they do) return Nothing. See the assert function for an
        example.
        Functions without an explicit return type return Nothing by default.
        Empty bodies return Nothing. If you really need a Nothing, you can just
        write curly braces.
      code
        type Nothing = struct {}
      code
        fun ignore[T](value: T) {}
    chapter Never
      text
        Never is a type with zero instances. If you write a function that
        accepts an argument of Never, it can never be called (otherwise, the
        caller would have a Never instance).
        Why do we need something like this? Some expressions always abort
        control flow, for example `panic("hi")`. These evaluate to the Never
        type. Because Never is never instantiated, it can be assigned to
        anything: `fibonacci(todo())`
      code
        type Never = enum {}
    chapter Dynamic
      text
        Dynamic holds an object whose type is not tracked by the type system.
        value.to_dynamic() forgets the static type; dynamic.to_static[T]() treats
        the object as T. Both conversions return the same heap object without
        copying it or adding a wrapper.
        to_static[T]() does not check the object's type. The programmer must
        supply the correct T; using the wrong type is undefined behavior.
      code
        type Dynamic = ...
        fun to_dynamic[T](value: T) Dynamic { ... }
        fun to_static[T](value: Dynamic) T { ... }
    chapter Bool
      text
        After the Never type with zero instances and the Nothing type with one
        instance, Norwegian scientists discovered a groundbreaking concept: A
        type with two instances!
      code
        type Bool = enum { false, true }
      text
        Please, please, please don't reassign.
      code
        var true = Bool.true
      code
        var false = Bool.false
      code
        fun not(a: Bool) Bool { if a then false else true }
      code
        fun ==(a: Bool, b: Bool) Bool { if a then b else not(b) }
        fun !=(a: Bool, b: Bool) Bool { not(a == b) }
      code
        fun implies(a: Bool, b: Bool) Bool { if a then b else true }
    chapter AndOr
      text
        The "and" and "or" keywords allow short-circuiting.

        Here are cases where the control flow short-circuits and the
        alternatives don't get evaluated:

        false and panic() | this is false
        true or panic()   | this is true
        some(2) or 3      | this is 2

        In these cases, the control flow doesn't short-circuit and the
        alternatives are evaluated as well:

        false or foo      | this is foo
        true and stuff    | this is stuff
        none[Int]() or 3  | this is 3

        The keywords turn into calls of left.and() or left.or(), which returns a
        ControlFlow. This logic flow determines whether the expression
        short-circuits or not.
      code
        type ControlFlow[A, B] = enum {
          short_circuit: A, evaluate_alternative: B
        }
      text
        Bools are short-circuiting.
      code
        fun and(bool: Bool) ControlFlow[Bool, Nothing] {
          if bool then
            ControlFlow[Bool, Nothing].evaluate_alternative
          else
            ControlFlow[Bool, Nothing].short_circuit(false)
        }
      code
        fun or(bool: Bool) ControlFlow[Bool, Nothing] {
          if bool then
            ControlFlow[Bool, Nothing].short_circuit(true)
          else
            ControlFlow[Bool, Nothing].evaluate_alternative
        }
    chapter Ordering
      text
        Consider implementing the spaceship operator <=>(T, T) Ordering for
        types that can be compared.
      code
        type Ordering = enum { less, equal, greater }
      code
        fun ==(a: Ordering, b: Ordering) Bool {
          a switch { less b is less equal b is equal greater b is greater }
        }
        fun flip(ordering: Ordering) Ordering {
          ordering switch {
            less Ordering.greater
            equal Ordering.equal
            greater Ordering.less
          }
        }
        fun is_less(o: Ordering) Bool { o is less }
        fun is_equal(o: Ordering) Bool { o is equal }
        fun is_greater(o: Ordering) Bool { o is greater }
        fun is_greater_equal(o: Ordering) Bool { not(o is less) }
        fun is_not_equal(o: Ordering) Bool { not(o is equal) }
        fun is_less_equal(o: Ordering) Bool { not(o is greater) }
    chapter Byte
      text
        A byte represents a number between 0 and 255, inclusive.

        Because Orchard's heap is word-based, a byte is really just a leaf
        object that takes up two words (header and data). So, for performance,
        you should store byte sequences as strings instead, only constructing
        Byte instances on demand (for example, when iterating over the
        sequence).
      code
        type Byte = ...
      text
        Byte arithmetic wraps into the range 0 to 255.
      code
        fun +(left: Byte, right: Byte) Byte { ... }
        fun -(left: Byte, right: Byte) Byte { ... }
        fun *(left: Byte, right: Byte) Byte { ... }
        fun /(left: Byte, right: Byte) Byte { ... }
        fun %(left: Byte, right: Byte) Byte { ... }
        fun &(left: Byte, right: Byte) Byte { ... }
        fun |(left: Byte, right: Byte) Byte { ... }
        fun xor(left: Byte, right: Byte) Byte { ... }
        fun <=>(left: Byte, right: Byte) Ordering { ... }
        fun ==(a: Byte, b: Byte) Bool { a <=> b is equal }
        fun <(a: Byte, b: Byte) Bool { a <=> b is less }
        fun >(a: Byte, b: Byte) Bool { a <=> b is greater }
        fun !=(a: Byte, b: Byte) Bool { not(a <=> b is equal) }
        fun >=(a: Byte, b: Byte) Bool { not(a <=> b is less) }
        fun <=(a: Byte, b: Byte) Bool { not(a <=> b is greater) }
    chapter String
      text
        A String is what you get from string literals such as "blub". There is
        no constraint that strings have to be valid UTF-8 or something like that
        – you can use them for arbitrary byte sequences.
      code
        type String = ...
    chapter Crashing
      text
        Crashing stops evaluation with a message.
      code
        fun crash(message: String) Never { ... }
        fun crash() Never { crash("crashed") }
      code
        fun unreachable() Never { crash("unreachable") }
      code
        fun todo() Never { crash("todo") }
    chapter Assert
      code
        fun assert(condition: Bool, message: String) {
          if not(condition) then crash(message)
        }
        fun assert(condition: Bool) { assert(condition, "assert failed") }
    chapter Maybe
      text
        Represents optional values. Like Option in Rust, but with the cooler
        Haskell name.
      code
        type Maybe[A] = enum { some: A, none }
      code
        fun some[A](value: A) Maybe[A] { Maybe.some(value) }
        fun none[A]() Maybe[A] { Maybe[A].none }
      code
        fun or[A](maybe: Maybe[A]) ControlFlow[A, Nothing] {
          maybe switch {
            some(a) ControlFlow[A, Nothing].short_circuit(a)
            none ControlFlow[A, Nothing].evaluate_alternative
          }
        }
      code
        fun map[A, B](maybe: Maybe[A], mapper: fun(A) B) Maybe[B] {
          maybe switch { some(a) some(mapper(a)) none none[B]() }
        }
        fun flatten[A](maybe: Maybe[Maybe[A]]) Maybe[A] { maybe or none[A]() }
        fun flat_map[A, B](maybe: Maybe[A], mapper: fun(A) Maybe[B]) Maybe[B] {
          maybe.map(mapper).flatten()
        }
        fun unwrap[A](maybe: Maybe[A], message: String) A {
          maybe or crash(message)
        }
        fun unwrap[A](maybe: Maybe[A]) A { maybe.unwrap("called none.unwrap") }
    chapter Result
      text
        The result of something that can either work or fail.
      code
        type Result[O, E] = enum { ok: O, error: E }
      code
        fun ok[O, E](o: O) Result[O, E] { Result[O, E].ok(o) }
        fun error[O, E](e: E) Result[O, E] { Result[O, E].error(e) }
      code
        fun or[O, E](result: Result[O, E]) ControlFlow[O, E] {
          result switch {
            ok(o) ControlFlow[O, E].short_circuit(o)
            error(e) ControlFlow[O, E].evaluate_alternative(e)
          }
        }
      code
        fun unwrap[O, E](result: Result[O, E], message: String) O {
          result switch { ok(o) o error crash(message) }
        }
        fun unwrap[O, E](result: Result[O, E]) O {
          result switch { ok(o) o error(e) crash("called error.unwrap") }
        }
      code
        fun map[O, E, A](result: Result[O, E], mapper: fun(O) A) Result[A, E] {
          result switch { ok(o) ok[A, E](mapper(o)) error(e) error[A, E](e) }
        }
        fun map_error[O, E, A](
          result: Result[O, E], mapper: fun(E) A
        ) Result[O, A] {
          result switch { ok(o) ok[O, A](o) error(e) error[O, A](mapper(e)) }
        }
    chapter Int
      text
        Ints are 64-bit signed integers. If you write a number such as 42 in the
        code, it's an Int.
      code
        type Int = ...
      code
        fun +(left: Int, right: Int) Int { ... }
        fun -(left: Int, right: Int) Int { ... }
        fun *(left: Int, right: Int) Int { ... }
        fun /(left: Int, right: Int) Int { ... }
        fun %(left: Int, right: Int) Int { ... }
        fun &(left: Int, right: Int) Int { ... }
        fun |(left: Int, right: Int) Int { ... }
        fun xor(left: Int, right: Int) Int { ... }
        fun negate(int: Int) Int { 0 - int }
        fun <=>(left: Int, right: Int) Ordering { ... }
        fun ==(a: Int, b: Int) Bool { a <=> b is equal }
        fun <(a: Int, b: Int) Bool { a <=> b is less }
        fun >(a: Int, b: Int) Bool { a <=> b is greater }
        fun !=(a: Int, b: Int) Bool { not(a <=> b is equal) }
        fun >=(a: Int, b: Int) Bool { not(a <=> b is less) }
        fun <=(a: Int, b: Int) Bool { not(a <=> b is greater) }
        fun <<(int: Int, by: Int) Int {
          if by == 0 then int else int * 2 << {by - 1}
        }
        fun >>(int: Int, by: Int) Int {
          if by == 0 then int else int / 2 << {by - 1}
        }
        fun abs(value: Int) Int { if value >= 0 then value else 0 - value }
        fun max(a: Int, b: Int) Int { if a > b then a else b }
        fun min(a: Int, b: Int) Int { if a < b then a else b }
        fun is_even(a: Int) Bool { a % 2 == 0 }
        fun is_odd(a: Int) Bool { a % 2 == 1 }
        fun pow(base: Int, exponent: Int) Int {
          if exponent == 0 then 1 else base * base.pow(exponent - 1)
        }
        fun round_up_to_multiple_of(number: Int, factor: Int) Int {
          number + factor - 1 / factor * factor
        }
        fun round_up_to_power_of(number: Int, base: Int) Int {
          number.round_up_to_power_of_rec(base, 1)
        }
        fun round_up_to_power_of_rec(
          number: Int, base: Int, candidate: Int
        ) Int {
          if candidate >= number then
            candidate
          else
            number.round_up_to_power_of_rec(base, candidate * base)
        }
      code
        fun sqrt(number: Int) Int {
          assert(
            number >= 0,
            "You can't take the square root of a negative number."
          )
          sqrt_rec(number, 0)
        }
        fun sqrt_rec(number: Int, candidate: Int) Int {
          var next = candidate + 1
          next * next <=> number switch {
            less sqrt_rec(number, next)
            equal next
            greater candidate
          }
        }
        fun log_2(value: Int) Int {
          if value == 1 then 0 else log_2(value / 2) + 1
        }
      text
        fun format(int: Int) String { int.format(10) }
        fun format(int: Int, radix: Int) String {
          if int < 0 then
            "-{int.negate().format(radix)}"
          else {
            concat(
              if int >= radix then format(int / radix, radix) else "",
              "0123456789abcdef".bytes().get(int % radix),
            )
          }
        }
      code
        # stuff for bytes
        fun lower_byte(value: Int) Byte { ... }
        fun to_int(value: Byte) Int { ... }
        fun to_byte(value: Int) Byte {
          assert(value >= 0 and value < 256)
          value.lower_byte()
        }
        fun <<(byte: Byte, by: Byte) Byte {
          {byte.to_int() << by.to_int()}.lower_byte()
        }
        fun >>(byte: Byte, by: Byte) Byte {
          {byte.to_int() >> by.to_int()}.lower_byte()
        }
        fun min(a: Byte, b: Byte) Byte { if a < b then a else b }
        fun max(a: Byte, b: Byte) Byte { if a > b then a else b }
      text
        fun format(byte: Byte) String { byte.to_int().format() }
      code
        var min_int = -9223372036854775808
        var max_int = 9223372036854775807
        var min_byte = lower_byte(0)
        var max_byte = lower_byte(255)
    chapter Float
      text
        Floats are 64-bit IEEE 754 floating point numbers. If you write a number
        such as 1.2 in the code, it's a Float.
      code
        type Float = ...
        fun to_float(value: Int) Float { ... }
        fun to_int(value: Float) Int { ... }
        fun +(left: Float, right: Float) Float { ... }
        fun -(left: Float, right: Float) Float { ... }
        fun *(left: Float, right: Float) Float { ... }
        fun /(left: Float, right: Float) Float { ... }
        fun <=>(left: Float, right: Float) Ordering { ... }
        fun ==(left: Float, right: Float) Bool { ... }
        fun !=(left: Float, right: Float) Bool { not(left == right) }
        fun <(left: Float, right: Float) Bool { ... }
        fun >(left: Float, right: Float) Bool { ... }
        fun <=(left: Float, right: Float) Bool { ... }
        fun >=(left: Float, right: Float) Bool { ... }
        fun abs(value: Float) Float {
          if value >= 0.0 then value else 0.0 - value
        }
        fun max(a: Float, b: Float) Float { if a > b then a else b }
        fun min(a: Float, b: Float) Float { if a < b then a else b }
    chapter Range
      text
        Ranges have an inclusive start and an exclusive end.
        All operators have the same precedence and associate from left to right.
      code
        type Range[A] = struct { start: A, end: A }
        fun ..[A](start: A, end: A) Range[A] { Range { start, end } }
        fun ..=(start: Int, end: Int) Range[Int] { start .. {end + 1} }
        fun ..+(start: Int, length: Int) Range[Int] {
          start .. {start + length}
        }
        fun ..+=(start: Int, length: Int) Range[Int] {
          start .. {start + length + 1}
        } 
        fun +-(mid: Int, distance: Int) Range[Int] {
          {mid - distance} .. {mid + distance + 1}
        }
        fun length(range: Range[Int]) Int { range.end - range.start }
        fun contains(range: Range[Int], value: Int) Bool {
          value >= range.start and value < range.end
        }
        fun contains(range: Range[Float], value: Float) Bool {
          value >= range.start and value < range.end
        }
        fun is_empty(range: Range[Int]) Bool { range.start >= range.end }
        fun is_not_empty(range: Range[Int]) Bool { not(range.is_empty()) }
      code
        type Infinity = struct {}
        var infinity = Infinity {}
      code
        type OpenRange[A] = struct { start: A }
        fun ..[A](start: A, infinity: Infinity) OpenRange[A] {
          OpenRange { start }
        }
    chapter LinkedList
      code
        type LinkedList[A] = enum { empty, more: More[A] }
        type More[A] = struct { head: A, tail: LinkedList[A] }
      code
        fun empty_linked_list[A]() LinkedList[A] { LinkedList[A].empty }
        fun cons[A](head: A, tail: LinkedList[A]) LinkedList[A] {
          LinkedList.more(More { head, tail })
        }
        fun push[A](list: LinkedList[A], item: A) LinkedList[A] {
          list switch {
            empty cons(item, empty_linked_list[A]())
            more(more) cons(more.head, more.tail.push(item))
          }
        }
        fun get[A](list: LinkedList[A], index: Int) A {
          list switch {
            empty crash("The index is out of bounds.")
            more(more) {
              if index == 0 then more.head else more.tail.get(index - 1)
            }
          }
        }
    chapter List
      text
        List[A] is an immutable array.
      code
        type List[A] = ...
        fun list[T]() List[T] { ... }
        fun length[T](list: List[T]) Int { ... }
        fun get[T](list: List[T], index: Int) T { ... }
        fun filled_list[T](length: Int, fill: T) List[T] { ... }
        fun push[T](list: List[T], item: T) List[T] { ... }
        fun push_all[T](list: List[T], items: List[T]) List[T] { ... }
        fun slice[T](list: List[T], range: Range[Int]) List[T] { ... }
        fun set[T](list: List[T], index: Int, item: T) List[T] { ... }
        fun reversed[T](list: List[T]) List[T] { ... }
        fun sum(list: List[Int]) Int { ... }
      code
        fun is_empty[A](list: List[A]) Bool { list.length() == 0 }
        fun is_not_empty[A](list: List[A]) Bool { not(list.is_empty()) }
        fun get_maybe[A](list: List[A], index: Int) Maybe[A] {
          if {0..list.length()}.contains(index)
          then some(list.get(index)) else none[A]()
        }
        fun list[A](a: A) List[A] { list[A]().push(a) }
        fun list[A](a: A, b: A) List[A] { list[A]().push(a).push(b) }
        fun list[A](a: A, b: A, c: A) List[A] {
          list[A]().push(a).push(b).push(c)
        }
        fun list[A](a: A, b: A, c: A, d: A) List[A] {
          list[A]().push(a).push(b).push(c).push(d)
        }
        fun list[A](a: A, b: A, c: A, d: A, e: A) List[A] {
          list[A]().push(a).push(b).push(c).push(d).push(e)
        }
        fun list[A](a: A, b: A, c: A, d: A, e: A, f: A) List[A] {
          list[A]().push(a).push(b).push(c).push(d).push(e).push(f)
        }
        fun list[A](a: A, b: A, c: A, d: A, e: A, f: A, g: A) List[A] {
          list[A]().push(a).push(b).push(c).push(d).push(e).push(f).push(g)
        }
        fun list[A](a: A, b: A, c: A, d: A, e: A, f: A, g: A, h: A) List[A] {
          list[A]().push(a).push(b).push(c).push(d).push(e).push(f).push(g)
            .push(h)
        }
        fun list[A](
          a: A, b: A, c: A, d: A, e: A, f: A, g: A, h: A, i: A
        ) List[A] {
          list[A]().push(a).push(b).push(c).push(d).push(e).push(f).push(g)
            .push(h).push(i)
        }
        fun list[A](
          a: A, b: A, c: A, d: A, e: A, f: A, g: A, h: A, i: A, j: A
        ) List[A] {
          list[A]().push(a).push(b).push(c).push(d).push(e).push(f).push(g)
            .push(h).push(i).push(j)
        }
      code
        fun first[A](list: List[A]) A { list.get(0) }
        fun last[A](list: List[A]) A { list.get(list.length() - 1) }
        fun first[A](list: List[A], amount: Int) List[A] {
          list.slice(0..amount)
        }
        fun last[A](list: List[A], amount: Int) List[A] {
          assert(
            amount >= 0 and amount <= list.length(),
            "List amount out of bounds.",
          )
          list.slice({list.length() - amount}..list.length())
        }
        fun without_first[A](list: List[A], amount: Int) List[A] {
          list.slice(amount..list.length())
        }
        fun without_first[A](list: List[A]) List[A] { list.without_first(1) }
        fun without_last[A](list: List[A], amount: Int) List[A] {
          assert(
            amount >= 0 and amount <= list.length(),
            "List amount out of bounds.",
          )
          list.slice(0..{list.length() - amount})
        }
        fun without_last[A](list: List[A]) List[A] { list.without_last(1) }
        fun pop[A](list: List[A]) List[A] {
          assert(list.is_not_empty(), "Cannot pop an empty list.")
          list.without_last()
        }
        fun first_maybe[A](list: List[A]) Maybe[A] { list.get_maybe(0) }
        fun last_maybe[A](list: List[A]) Maybe[A] {
          list.get_maybe(list.length() - 1)
        }
        fun insert[A](list: List[A], index: Int, item: A) List[A] {
          list.first(index).push(item).push_all(list.without_first(index))
        }
        fun remove[A](list: List[A], range: Range[Int]) List[A] {
          assert(
            0 <= range.start
              and range.start <= range.end
              and range.end <= list.length(),
            "List range out of bounds.",
          )
          list.first(range.start).push_all(list.without_first(range.end))
        }
    chapter StringBytes
      text
        bytes returns the string's UTF-8 bytes.
        TODO: Instead of eagerly constructing a giant array, make this return an
        iterator?
      code
        fun bytes(value: String) List[Byte] { ... }
      code
        fun byte(string: String) Byte {
          var b = string.bytes()
          if b.length() == 1 then b.get(0) else crash("String is too long.")
        }
      code
        fun parse_int(string: String, radix: Int) Int {
          string.bytes().parse_int(radix)
        }
        fun parse_int(bytes: List[Byte], radix: Int) Int {
          var digit = bytes.get(0).parse_digit(radix)
          if bytes.length() == 1 then
            digit
          else
            digit * radix + bytes.without_first(1).parse_int(radix)
        }
        # TODO: throw an error if the string contains a digit above the radix
        fun parse_digit(char: Byte) Int {
          if char >= byte("0") and value <= byte("9") then
            value - byte("0")
          else if value >= byte("a") and value <= byte("f") then
            value - byte("a") + 10
          else if value >= byte("A") and value <= byte("F") then
            value - byte("f") + 10
          else
            crash("Invalid digit")
        }
    chapter Tuples
      code
        type Tuple2[A, B] = struct { a: A, b: B }
        type Tuple3[A, B, C] = struct { a: A, b: B, c: C }
      code
        fun tuple[A, B](a: A, b: B) Tuple2[A, B] { Tuple2 { a, b } }
        fun tuple[A, B, C](a: A, b: B, c: C) Tuple3[A, B, C] {
          Tuple3 { a, b, c }
        }
    chapter Iterators
      text
        Iterators are defined by their behavior (you can get the next item)
        rather than their structure. That's why iterators (Iter for short) just
        contain a function pointer.
      code
        type Iter[A] = struct { next: fun() Maybe[Next[A]] }
        type Next[A] = struct { item: A, rest: Iter[A] }
      code
        fun iter_from_fun[A](next: fun() Maybe[Next[A]]) Iter[A] {
          Iter { next }
        }
      code
        type NextState[A, S] = struct { item: A, state: S }
        fun iter_from_state[A, S](
          state: S, next: fun(S) Maybe[NextState[A, S]]
        ) Iter[A] {
          iter_from_fun(fun() {
            next(state).map(fun(stuff: NextState[A, S]) {
              Next {
                item = stuff.item, rest = iter_from_state(stuff.state, next)
              }
            })
          })
        }
      code
        fun is_empty[A](iter: Iter[A]) Bool { iter.next() is none }
      code
        fun filter_map[A, B](iter: Iter[A], mapper: fun(A) Maybe[B]) Iter[B] {
          iter_from_fun(fun() {
            iter.next().flat_map(fun(next: Next[A]) {
              mapper(next.item) switch {
                none next.rest.filter_map(mapper).next()
                some(b) {
                  some(Next { item = b, rest = next.rest.filter_map(mapper) })
                }
              }
            })
          })
        }
      code
        fun map[A, B](iter: Iter[A], mapper: fun(A) B) Iter[B] {
          iter_from_fun(fun() {
            iter.next().map(fun(next: Next[A]) {
              Next { item = mapper(next.item), rest = map(next.rest, mapper) }
            })
          })
        }
      code
        type Indexed[A] = struct { index: Int, item: A }
        fun indexed[A](iter: Iter[A]) Iter[Indexed[A]] { iter.indexed_rec(0) }
        fun indexed_rec[A](iter: Iter[A], start: Int) Iter[Indexed[A]] {
          iter_from_fun(fun() {
            iter.next().map(fun(next: Next[A]) {
              Next {
                item = Indexed { index = start, item = next.item },
                rest = next.rest.indexed_rec(start + 1),
              }
            })
          })
        }
      code
        fun map_indexed[A, B](iter: Iter[A], mapper: fun(Int, A) B) Iter[B] {
          iter.indexed().map(fun(indexed: Indexed[A]) {
            mapper(indexed.index, indexed.item)
          })
        }
      code
        fun filter[A](iter: Iter[A], checker: fun(A) Bool) Iter[A] {
          iter_from_fun(fun() {
            iter.next() switch {
              none none[Next[A]]()
              some(next) {
                if checker(next.item) then
                  some(Next {
                    item = next.item, rest = next.rest.filter(checker)
                  })
                else
                  next.rest.filter(checker).next()
              }
            }
          })
        }
      code
        fun fold[A, S](iter: Iter[A], state: S, folder: fun(S, A) S) S {
          iter.next() switch {
            none state
            some(next) next.rest.fold(folder(state, next.item), folder)
          }
        }
      code
        fun reduce[A](iter: Iter[A], reducer: fun(A, A) A) Maybe[A] {
          iter.next() switch {
            none none[A]()
            some(next) some(next.rest.fold(next.item, reducer))
          }
        }
      code
        fun all[A](iter: Iter[A], check: fun(A) Bool) Bool {
          iter.next() switch {
            none true
            some(next) check(next.item) and next.rest.all(check)
          }
        }
      code
        fun any[A](iter: Iter[A], check: fun(A) Bool) Bool {
          iter.next() switch {
            none false
            some(next) check(next.item) or next.rest.any(check)
          }
        }
      code
        fun skip[A](iter: Iter[A], amount: Int) Iter[A] {
          iter_from_fun(fun() {
            iter.next().flat_map(fun(next: Next[A]) {
              next.rest.skip(amount - 1).next()
            })
          })
        }
      code
        fun skip_while[A](iter: Iter[A], checker: fun(A) Bool) Iter[A] {
          iter_from_fun(fun() {
            iter.next() switch {
              none none[Next[A]]()
              some(next) {
                if checker(next.item) then
                  next.rest.skip_while(checker).next()
                else
                  some(next)
              }
            }
          })
        }
      code
        fun take[A](iter: Iter[A], amount: Int) Iter[A] {
          iter_from_fun(fun() {
            if amount == 0 then
              none[Next[A]]()
            else
              iter.next().map(fun(next: Next[A]) {
                Next { item = next.item, rest = next.rest.take(amount - 1) }
              })
          })
        }
      code
        fun take_while[A](iter: Iter[A], checker: fun(A) Bool) Iter[A] {
          iter_from_fun(fun() {
            iter.next() switch {
              none none[Next[A]]()
              some(next) {
                if checker(next.item) then
                  some(Next {
                    item = next.item, rest = next.rest.take_while(checker)
                  })
                else
                  none[Next[A]]()
              }
            }
          })
        }
      code
        # Zips two iters of equal length.
        fun zip[A, B](a: Iter[A], b: Iter[B]) Iter[Tuple2[A, B]] {
          iter_from_fun(fun() {
            a.next() switch {
              none {
                assert(b.next() is none, "zip iters have different lengths")
                none[Next[Tuple2[A, B]]]()
              }
              some(aa) {
                b.next() switch {
                  none crash("zip iters have different lengths")
                  some(bb) {
                    some(Next {
                      item = tuple(aa.item, bb.item),
                      rest = zip(aa.rest, bb.rest),
                    })
                  }
                }
              }
            }
          })
        }
      code
        fun zip_short[A, B](a: Iter[A], b: Iter[B]) Iter[Tuple2[A, B]] {
          iter_from_fun(fun() {
            a.next().flat_map(fun(aa: Next[A]) {
              b.next().map(fun(bb: Next[B]) {
                Next {
                  item = tuple(aa.item, bb.item),
                  rest = zip_short(aa.rest, bb.rest),
                }
              })
            })
          })
        }
      code
        fun get_maybe[A](iter: Iter[A], index: Int) Maybe[A] {
          iter.skip(index).next().map(fun(next: Next[A]) { next.item })
        }
      text
        fun get[A](iter: Iter[A], index: Int) A {
          iter.get_maybe(index)
            or crash("Index {index.format()} is out of bounds.")
        }
      code
        fun last[A](iter: Iter[A]) Maybe[A] {
          iter.next().map(fun(next: Next[A]) {
            next.rest.last_rec(next.item)
          })
        }
        fun last_rec[A](iter: Iter[A], previous: A) A {
          iter.next() switch {
            none previous
            some(next) next.rest.last_rec(next.item)
          }
        }
      code
        fun chain[A](a: Iter[A], b: Iter[A]) Iter[A] {
          iter_from_fun(fun() {
            a.next() switch {
              none b.next()
              some(next) {
                some(Next { item = next.item, rest = next.rest.chain(b) })
              }
            }
          })
        }
      code
        fun flat_map[A, B](iter: Iter[A], mapper: fun(A) Iter[B]) Iter[B] {
          iter_from_fun(fun() {
            iter.next().flat_map(fun(next: Next[A]) {
              mapper(next.item).chain(next.rest.flat_map(mapper)).next()
            })
          })
        }
      code
        fun flatten[A](iter: Iter[Iter[A]]) Iter[A] {
          iter.flat_map(fun(it: Iter[A]) { it })
        }
      code
        fun cycle[A](iter: Iter[A]) Iter[A] { iter.chain(iter.cycle()) }
      code
        fun length[A](iter: Iter[A]) Int {
          iter.fold(0, fun(count: Int, item: A) { count + 1 })
        }
      code
        fun sum(iter: Iter[Int]) Int {
          iter.fold(0, fun(a: Int, b: Int) { a + b })
        }
      code
        fun min(iter: Iter[Int]) Maybe[Int] {
          iter.reduce(fun(a: Int, b: Int) { min(a, b) })
        }
      code
        fun max(iter: Iter[Int]) Maybe[Int] {
          iter.reduce(fun(a: Int, b: Int) { max(a, b) })
        }
      code
        fun iter(range: Range[Int]) Iter[Int] {
          iter_from_state(range.start, fun(next: Int) {
            if next == range.end then
              none[NextState[Int, Int]]()
            else
              some(NextState { item = next, state = next + 1 })
          })
        }
      code
        fun iter(range: OpenRange[Int]) Iter[Int] {
          iter_from_state(range.start, fun(next: Int) {
            some(NextState { item = next, state = next + 1 })
          })
        }
      code
        fun iter[T](list: List[T]) Iter[T] {
          iter_from_state(0, fun(index: Int) {
            if index == list.length() then
              none[NextState[T, Int]]()
            else
              some(NextState { item = list.get(index), state = index + 1 })
          })
        }
        fun to_list[T](iter: Iter[T]) List[T] {
          iter.fold(list[T](), fun(list: List[T], item: T) { list.push(item) })
        }
    chapter Hashing
      code
        type Hash = struct { value: Int }
      code
        fun combine(a: Hash, b: Hash) Hash {
          Hash { value = a.value * 33 + b.value }
        }
        fun combine_commutative(a: Hash, b: Hash) Hash {
          Hash { value = xor(a.value, b.value) }
        }
      code
        fun hash(int: Int) Hash { Hash { value = int } }
      code
        fun hash(bool: Bool) Hash { {if bool then 1 else 0}.hash() }
      code
        fun hash(byte: Byte) Hash { byte.to_int().hash() }
      code
        fun hash[A](iter: Iter[A], hash_item: fun(A) Hash) Hash {
          iter.fold(5381.hash(), fun(hash: Hash, item: A) {
            combine(hash, hash_item(item))
          })
        }
      code
        fun hash_commutative[A](iter: Iter[A], hash_item: fun(A) Hash) Hash {
          iter.fold(5381.hash(), fun(hash: Hash, item: A) {
            combine_commutative(hash, hash_item(item))
          })
        }
      code
        fun hash[A](list: List[A], hash_item: fun(A) Hash) Hash {
          combine(list.length().hash(), list.iter().hash(hash_item))
        }
    chapter Formatting
      code
        fun format(bool: Bool) String { if bool then "true" else "false" }
        fun format(o: Ordering) String {
          o switch { less "less" equal "equal" greater "greater" }
        }
  chapter UserInterface
    text
      Orchard supports two-dimensional graphics. The coordinate system starts at
      the top left with (0, 0) and coordinates generally use Floats. All
      graphics are vector-based and use the sRGB color space.
    chapter Color
      text
        Orchard uses the sRGB color space.
      code
        type Color = struct { r: Byte, g: Byte, b: Byte }
        fun color(r: Byte, g: Byte, b: Byte) Color { Color { r, g, b } }
      code
        fun color(hex: String) Color {
          var digits = hex.bytes()
          assert(digits.length() == 7, "Expected #RRGGBB")
          assert(digits.get(0).to_int() == 35, "Expected #RRGGBB")
          color(
            digits.slice(1..3).parse_int(16).to_byte(),
            digits.slice(3..5).parse_int(16).to_byte(),
            digits.slice(5..7).parse_int(16).to_byte(),
          )
        }
      text
        fun format(color: Color) String {
          "#
            '{color.r.format(16).pad_left(2, "0")}
            '{color.g.format(16).pad_left(2, "0")}
            '{color.b.format(16).pad_left(2, "0")}"
        }
    chapter Vec2
      text
        A mathematical two-dimensional vector with no meaning associated to
        the components.
      code
        type Vec2[A] = struct { x: A, y: A }
        fun vec[A](x: A, y: A) Vec2[A] { Vec2 { x, y } }
      text
        Operations specifically for vectors of floating-point numbers.
      code
        fun +(a: Vec2[Float], b: Vec2[Float]) Vec2[Float] {
          Vec2 { x = a.x + b.x, y = a.y + b.y }
        }
        fun -(a: Vec2[Float], b: Vec2[Float]) Vec2[Float] {
          Vec2 { x = a.x - b.x, y = a.y - b.y }
        }
        fun *(f: Float, a: Vec2[Float]) Vec2[Float] {
          Vec2 { x = f * a.x, y = f * a.y }
        }
        fun *(a: Vec2[Float], f: Float) Vec2[Float] { f * a }
        fun /(a: Vec2[Float], f: Float) Vec2[Float] {
          Vec2 { x = a.x / f, y = a.y / f }
        }
    chapter Offset
      text
        An offset in a two-dimensional plane.
        TODO: Do I need Offset when I have position and size?
      code
        type Offset = struct { x: Float, y: Float }
        fun offset(x: Float, y: Float) Offset { Offset { x, y } }
        fun to_vec(offset: Offset) Vec2[Float] { vec(offset.x, offset.y) }
        fun to_offset(vec: Vec2[Float]) Offset { offset(vec.x, vec.y) }
        fun +(a: Offset, b: Offset) Offset {
          {a.to_vec() + b.to_vec()}.to_offset()
        }
        fun -(a: Offset, b: Offset) Offset {
          {a.to_vec() + b.to_vec()}.to_offset()
        }
        fun *(f: Float, a: Offset) Offset { {f * a.to_vec()}.to_offset() }
        fun *(a: Offset, f: Float) Offset { {a.to_vec() * f}.to_offset() }
        fun /(a: Offset, f: Float) Offset { {a.to_vec() / f}.to_offset() }
    chapter Position
      text
        A position in a two-dimensional plane.
      code
        type Position = struct { x: Float, y: Float }
        fun position(x: Float, y: Float) Position { Position { x, y } }
        fun to_vec(pos: Position) Vec2[Float] { vec(pos.x, pos.y) }
        fun to_position(vec: Vec2[Float]) Position { position(vec.x, vec.y) }
        fun +(pos: Position, offset: Offset) Position {
          {pos.to_vec() + offset.to_vec()}.to_position()
        }
        fun -(pos: Position, offset: Offset) Position {
          {pos.to_vec() - offset.to_vec()}.to_position()
        }
    chapter Size
      text
        A two-dimensional size.
      code
        type Size = struct { width: Float, height: Float }
        fun size(width: Float, height: Float) Size { Size { width, height } }
        fun to_vec(size: Size) Vec2[Float] { vec(size.width, size.height) }
        fun to_size(vec: Vec2[Float]) Size { size(vec.x, vec.y) }
        fun *(f: Float, size: Size) Size { {f * size.to_vec()}.to_size() }
        fun *(size: Size, f: Float) Size { {size.to_vec() * f}.to_size() }
        fun /(size: Size, f: Float) Size { {size.to_vec() / f}.to_size() }
    chapter Rectangle
      code
        type Rectangle = struct {
          left: Float, top: Float, right: Float, bottom: Float
        }
        fun rectangle(
          left: Float, top: Float, right: Float, bottom: Float
        ) Rectangle {
          Rectangle { left, top, right, bottom }
        }
        fun rectangle(top_left: Position, size: Size) Rectangle {
          rectangle(
            top_left.x,
            top_left.y,
            top_left.x + size.width,
            top_left.y + size.height,
          )
        }
        fun top_left(rect: Rectangle) Position {
          position(rect.left, rect.top)
        }
        fun top_right(rect: Rectangle) Position {
          position(rect.right, rect.top)
        }
        fun bottom_left(rect: Rectangle) Position {
          position(rect.left, rect.bottom)
        }
        fun bottom_right(rect: Rectangle) Position {
          position(rect.right, rect.bottom)
        }
        fun center(rect: Rectangle) Position {
          position(
            {rect.left + rect.right} / 2.0, {rect.top + rect.bottom} / 2.0
          )
        }
        fun contains(rect: Rectangle, pos: Position) Bool {
          {rect.left .. rect.right}.contains(pos.x)
            and {rect.top .. rect.bottom}.contains(pos.y)
        }
        fun intersection(a: Rectangle, b: Rectangle) Maybe[Rectangle] {
          var left = max(a.left, b.left)
          var top = max(a.top, b.top)
          var right = min(a.right, b.right)
          var bottom = min(a.bottom, b.bottom)
          if left < right and top < bottom then
            some(rectangle(left, top, right, bottom))
          else
            none[Rectangle]()
        }
    chapter Drawings
      text
        When Orchard code is asked to render something for the outside world,
        it returns draw commands. Think of these similar to the elements you
        would find in SVGs.
      chapter HistoricalNote
        text
          Initially, I tried to communicate with the outside world using pixel
          graphics. However, I believe that's not the right move as pixels are
          more difficult to handle in code. Having pixels (or some concept of a
          texture) requires either:
          - mutable values (which Orchard doesn't have), or
          - lots of copying on every texture modification (meh), or
          - a super-smart optimizing compiler (which is useful but it has the
            sort of slow-by-default-and-you-have-to-trust-the-black-box-to-work
            feeling I don't like), or
          - inefficient storage of individual pixels in trees.
          By communicating the intent on a quite high-level, we can instead use
          GPUs when turning the high-level draw commands into pixels.
          This is also similar to how Flutter works: The Dart VM is decently
          fast, but not super fast, so it just emits draw commands that
          the lower-level C++ engine takes and turns into pixels.
      chapter Transformations
        text
          Transformation matrices ... exist. They have this form:
          a b c
          d e f
          TODO: better description
        code
          type TransformationMatrix = struct {
            a: Float, b: Float, c: Float, d: Float, e: Float, f: Float
          }
          fun transformation_matrix(
            a: Float, b: Float, c: Float, d: Float, e: Float, f: Float
          ) TransformationMatrix {
            TransformationMatrix { a, b, c, d, e, f }
          }
          fun transform_translate(offset: Offset) TransformationMatrix {
            transformation_matrix(1.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0, offset.x, offset.y)
          }
          fun transform_scale(factor: Float) TransformationMatrix {
            transformation_matrix(factor, 0.0, 0.0, factor, 0.0, 0.0)
          }
      chapter Paths
        code
          type PathSegment = enum { move_to: Position, line_to: Position }
          type Path = struct { segments: List[PathSegment] }
        code
          fun path() Path { Path { segments = list[PathSegment]() } }
          fun move_to(path: Path, pos: Position) Path {
            Path { segments = path.segments.push(PathSegment.move_to(pos)) }
          }
          fun move_to(path: Path, x: Float, y: Float) Path {
            path.move_to(position(x, y))
          }
          fun line_to(path: Path, pos: Position) Path {
            Path { segments = path.segments.push(PathSegment.line_to(pos)) }
          }
          fun line_to(path: Path, x: Float, y: Float) Path {
            path.line_to(position(x, y))
          }
      code
        type DrawCommand = enum {
          nothing,
          fill_path: FillPath,
          transform: Transform,
          all: List[DrawCommand],
        }
        type FillPath = struct { path: Path, color: Color }
        type Transform = struct { by: TransformationMatrix, what: DrawCommand }
      code
        fun draw_nothing() DrawCommand { DrawCommand.nothing }
        fun draw_filled_path(path: Path, color: Color) DrawCommand {
          DrawCommand.fill_path(FillPath { path, color })
        }
        fun draw_transformed(
          by: TransformationMatrix, what: DrawCommand
        ) DrawCommand {
          DrawCommand.transform(Transform { by, what })
        }
        fun draw_all(commands: List[DrawCommand]) DrawCommand {
          DrawCommand.all(commands)
        }
        fun draw_colored_rect(size: Size, color: Color) DrawCommand {
          draw_filled_path(
            path()
              .move_to(0.0, 0.0)
              .line_to(size.width, 0.0)
              .line_to(size.width, size.height)
              .line_to(0.0, size.height),
            color,
          )
        }
      code
        type Drawing = struct { draw: DrawCommand, size: Size }
    chapter Apps
      text
        A notebook exposes its UI as a variable named app. Rendering receives
        the current window size; handling an event returns the next app.
      code
        type KeyPressed = struct {
          keycode: Int, control: Bool, shift: Bool, alt: Bool
        }
        type CharEntered = struct { codepoint: Int }
        type Event = enum {
          window_resized: Size,
          key_pressed: KeyPressed,
          char_entered: CharEntered,
          mouse_clicked: Position,
          mouse_moved: Position,
        }
        type App = struct {
          render: fun(Size) DrawCommand,
          handle: fun(Event) App,
        }
    chapter CrosshairApp
      text
        Four colored triangles share the mouse position as their third vertex.
        Each triangle spans one edge of the window. Moving the mouse moves
        their meeting point; resizing keeps their bases on the window edges.
      code
        fun draw_triangle(
          a: Position, b: Position, c: Position, color: Color
        ) DrawCommand {
          draw_filled_path(
            path().move_to(a).line_to(b).line_to(c),
            color,
          )
        }
        fun crosshair_app(mouse: Position) App {
          App {
            render = fun(window: Size) {
              var top_left = position(0.0, 0.0)
              var top_right = position(window.width, 0.0)
              var bottom_right = position(window.width, window.height)
              var bottom_left = position(0.0, window.height)
              draw_all(list(
                draw_triangle(top_left, top_right, mouse,
                  color(48.to_byte(), 96.to_byte(), 160.to_byte())),
                draw_triangle(top_right, bottom_right, mouse,
                  color(224.to_byte(), 112.to_byte(), 80.to_byte())),
                draw_triangle(bottom_right, bottom_left, mouse,
                  color(48.to_byte(), 160.to_byte(), 128.to_byte())),
                draw_triangle(bottom_left, top_left, mouse,
                  color(224.to_byte(), 176.to_byte(), 64.to_byte())),
              ))
            },
            handle = fun(event: Event) {
              event switch {
                mouse_moved(pos) crosshair_app(pos)
                default crosshair_app(mouse)
              }
            },
          }
        }
        var app = crosshair_app(position(250.0, 150.0))
    text
      WidgetFramework
    chapter WidgetFramework
      text
        The widget framework works similar to Flutter: You define your UI by
        nesting tiny, simple widgets in a declarative way. The framework turns
        this widget tree into an element tree. Elements are persistent nodes
        with internal state. On the next frame, you rebuild a widget tree and
        the framework updates the element tree accordingly.
      text
        Constraints (min/max width/height) flow down the tree and concrete
        renderings with concrete sizes flow up the tree. Parent widgets
        determine the position of child widgets.
      chapter Constraints
        code
          type AxisConstraints = struct { min: Float, max: Maybe[Float] }
          type Constraints { width: AxisConstraints, height: AxisConstraints }
        code
          fun tight_axis_constraints(size: Float) AxisConstraints {
            AxisConstraints { min = size, max = some(size) }
          }
          fun tight_constraints(size: Size) Constraints {
            Constraints {
              width = tight_axis_constraints(size.width),
              height = tight_axis_constraints(size.height),
            }
          }
          fun loosen_min(axis: AxisConstraints) AxisConstraints {
            AxisConstraints { min = 0, max = axis.max }
          }
          fun loosen_min(constraints: Constraints) Constraints {
            Constraints {
              width = constraints.width.loosen_min(),
              height = constraints.height.loosen_min(),
            }
          }
          fun min(constraints: Constraints) Size {
            size(constraints.width.min, constraints.height.min)
          }
          fun max(constraints: Constraints) Maybe[Size] {
            constraints.width.max.map(fun(width: Float) {
              constraints.height.max.map(fun(height: Float) {
                size(width, height)
              })
            })
          }
        code
          fun clamp(number: Float, low: Float, high: Float) Float {
            min(high, max(low, number))
          }
          fun clamp(number: Float, constraints: AxisConstraints) Float {
            constraints.max switch {
              none max(number, constraints.min)
              some(max) number.clamp(constraints.min, max)
            }
          }
          fun clamp(size: Size, constraints: Constraints) Size {
            size(
              size.width.clamp(constraints.width),
              size.height.clamp(constraints.height),
            )
          }
      chapter Widget
        text
          Previously, I just had functions that return Drawings, for example a
          text function that takes a String and returns a rendered version.
          However, this is not performant (you have to re-layout and re-render
          everything every frame) and having internal state is generally useful.
          For example, if you have a text in a scrollable view, you don't want
          to re-layout the text every frame, but cache its draw commands.
        code
          type Widget[M] = struct {
            new: fun(Constraints) Element[M],
          }
          type Element[M] = struct {
            constraints: Constraints,
            size: Size,
            drawing: Drawing,
            update: fun(Widget[M]) Element[M],
            new_constraints: fun(Constraints) Element[M],
            mouse_clicked: fun(Position) MouseClickResult[M],
          }
          type MouseClickResult[M] = enum {
            not_handled, handled: Element[M], message: M
          }
        code
          type WidgetImpl[M, W, E] = struct {
            new: fun(widget: W, constraints: Constraints) E,
            update: fun(element: E, widget: W) E,
            new_constraints: fun(element: E, constraints: Constraints) E,
            mouse_clicked: fun(element: E, position: Position) MouseClickResult[M],
            render: fun(e: E) Drawing,
          }
          fun widget[M, W, E](
            impl: WidgetImpl[M, W, E], current: W
          ) Widget[M] {
            Widget {
              new = fun(constraints: Constraints) {
                ...
              },
            }
          }
      chapter EmptyWidget
        code
          var empty_widget = widget[Never, Size](
            fun(constraints: Constraints) { constraints.min() },
            fun(state: Size) { Drawing { draw = draw_nothing(), size } },
            fun(size: Size, mouse: Position) { MouseClickResult.not_handled },
          )
      chapter ColoredWidget
        code
          type ColoredWidget[M] = struct { color: Color, child: Widget[M] }
          type ColoredElement[M] = struct { color: Color, child: Element[M] }
          fun colored_impl[M](
          ) WidgetImpl[M, ColoredWidget[M], ColoredElement[M]] {
            WidgetImpl {
              new = fun(w: ColoredWidget[M], constraints: Constraints) {
                ColoredElement {
                  color = w.color, child = w.child.create_element(constraints)
                }
              },
              update = fun(e: ColoredElement[M], widget: ColoredWidget) {
                ColoredElement {
                  color = widget.color, child = e.child.update(widget.child)
                }
              },
              new_constraints = fun(
                e: ColoredElement[M], constraints: Constraints
              ) {
                ColoredElement {
                  color = e.color,
                  child = e.child.new_constraints(constraints),
                }
              },
              mouse_clicked = fun(e: ColoredElement[M], position: Position) {
                e.child.mouse_clicked(position) switch {
                  not_handled MouseClickResult.not_handled
                  handled(new_child) {
                    MouseClickResult.handled(ColoredElement {
                      color = e.color, child = new_child
                    })
                  }
                  message(m) MouseClickResult.message(m)
                }
              },
              render = fun(e: ColoredElement[M]) {
                Drawing {
                  draw = draw_all(list(
                    draw_colored_rect(e.color, e.child.size),
                    e.child.drawing.draw,
                  )),
                  size = e.child.size,
                }
              }
            }
          }
          fun colored[M](color: Color, child: Widget[M]) Widget[M] {
            widget(colored_impl[M](), ColoredWidget { color, child })
          }
      chapter Align
        code
          type AlignWidget[M] = struct { bias: Vec2[Float], child: Widget[M] }
          type AlignElement[M] = struct {
            bias: Vec2[Float], child: Element[M], constraints: Constraints
          }
          fun align_impl[M](
          ) WidgetImpl[M, AlignWidget[M], AlignElement[M]] {
            var calculate_size = fun(
              child_size: Size, constraints: Constraints
            ) {
              size(
                constraints.width.max or child_size.width,
                constraints.height.max or child_size.height,
              )
            }
            WidgetImpl {
              new = fun(w: AlignWidget[M], constraints: Constraints) {
                AlignElement {
                  bias = w.bias,
                  child = w.child.create_element(constraints),
                  constraints,
                }
              },
              update = fun(e: AlignElement[M], w: AlignWidget) {
                AlignElement {
                  bias = w.bias,
                  child = e.child.update(w.child),
                  constraints = e.constraints,
                }
              },
              new_constraints = fun(
                e: AlignElement[M], constraints: Constraints
              ) {
                AlignElement {
                  bias = e.bias,
                  child = e.child.new_constraints(constraints.loosen_min()),
                  constraints,
                }
              },
              mouse_clicked = fun(e: AlignElement[M], position: Position) {
                var size = calculate_size(e.child.size, e.constraints)
                var padding = state.bias
                  * (size.to_vec() - e.child.size.to_vec())
                var position_in_child = position - padding
                if rect(0.0 @ 0.0, child.size).contains(position_in_child) then
                  e.child.mouse_clicked(position_in_child) switch {
                    not_handled MouseClickResult.not_handled
                    handled(new_child) {
                      MouseClickResult.handled(AlignElement {
                        horizontal = e.horizontal,
                        vertical = e.vertical,
                        child = new_child,
                        constraints = e.constraints,
                      })
                    }
                    message(m) MouseClickResult.message(m)
                  }
                else
                  MouseClickResult.not_handled
              },
              render = fun(e: AlignElement[M]) {
                var size = calculate_size(e.child.size, e.constraints)
                Drawing {
                  draw = draw_transformed(
                    transform_translate(
                      {e.bias * {size.to_vec() - e.child.size.to_vec()}}
                        .to_offset()
                    ),
                    child.drawing.draw,
                  ),
                  size,
                }
              }
            }
          }
          fun align[M](
            horizontal: Float, vertical: Float, child: Widget[M]
          ) Widget[M] {
            widget(
              align_impl[M](),
              AlignWidget { bias = vec(horizontal, vertical), child },
            )
          }
          fun center[M](child: Widget[M]) Widget[M] { align(0.0, 0.0, child) }
